          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(x)=x^3-x+1",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q39-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Divide $P(x)$ by $(x-1)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q(x)=x+2$, $R(x)=2x-1$.",
          "worked": "$ (x-1)^2=x^2-2x+1 $. Successive subtraction gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^3-x+1-x(x^2-2x+1)&=2x^2-2x+1\\\\\n2x^2-2x+1-2(x^2-2x+1)&=2x-1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$P(x)=(x-1)^2(x+2)+(2x-1).$$\nThe quotient is $x+2$ and the remainder is $2x-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q39-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the tangent line to $y=P(x)$ at $x=1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2x-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\nP(1)&=1^3-1+1=1\\\\\nP'(x)&=3x^2-1\\\\\nP'(1)&=3(1)^2-1=2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe tangent passes through $(1,1)$ with gradient $2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\ny-1&=2(x-1)\\\\\ny&=2x-1.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q39-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Verify that the remainder from part (a) equals the tangent line from part (b).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$R(x)=2x-1$, so $y=R(x)$ is the tangent $y=2x-1$.",
          "worked": "Part (a) gives $R(x)=2x-1$. Part (b) gives the tangent $y=2x-1$. Therefore $y=R(x)$ is the same line.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q39-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Let $R(x)$ be the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-r)^2$. Show that $y=R(x)$ is tangential to $y=P(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p8-q39-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "R(x)=P'(r)(x-r)+P(r)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", the tangent at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=r",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p8-q39-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Differentiate the division identity and evaluate it and its derivative at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=r",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The remainder has degree at most $1$, so write $R(x)=A(x-r)+B$. By the division transformation,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP(x)&=(x-r)^2Q(x)+A(x-r)+B\\\\\nP(r)&=B.\n\\end{align*}$$\nDifferentiating and evaluating at $x=r$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP'(x)&=2(x-r)Q(x)+(x-r)^2Q'(x)+A\\\\\nP'(r)&=A.\n\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$R(x)=P'(r)(x-r)+P(r).$$\nThus $y=R(x)$ passes through $(r,P(r))$ and has gradient $P'(r)$, so it is the tangent to $y=P(x)$ at $x=r$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
  },
